# The heavy commands a turn runs itself (#1298): citadel-test, citadel-heavy's local runs, ui-shot's browser and dev
# copies, each in a scope of its own (tools/heavy-unit), under citadel.slice beside citadel-turns.slice and
# citadel-builds.slice. A turn's unit stops as a whole when the kernel OOM-kills anything in it (OOMPolicy=stop), so a
# test or a browser that ran out of memory used to end its turn ("the turn's process was killed"). Here only that
# command dies, and the turn reads heavy-unit's message and retries (Cara, 2026-11-20: "I want to be able to just kick
# off as many minions as I want ... if the individual jobs take longer or whatever because of CPU or Memory constraints
# stopping real work, thats ok").
# MemoryHigh throttles them first; past MemoryMax the kernel kills inside this slice, never in a turn. systemd-oomd
# kills a heavy command here sooner than anywhere else in Citadel, past 60% memory pressure for 20 s (#1338: its
# drop-in, citadel-heavy.slice.d/, and deploy/oomd.md): heavy work gives way before turns slow down much.
# Low CPU and IO weight: turns, the server and ships get the cores first; heavy work takes what's left.
# Starting numbers (#1298), not measured yet: single-file runs and a browser take well under 1G each, and about one to
# four run at once; cargo and gradle builds go to CI first (#1160). Tune from #1280's storm records on the Status page.
# Not installed yet, the slice still works, with no limit of its own.
[Unit]
Description=Heavy commands of Citadel's turns

[Slice]
MemoryHigh=8G
MemoryMax=10G
CPUWeight=20
IOWeight=20
